<BaseLayout title="New Year on the East Coast — Huanyu Chen"
  description="A week around New Year's 2023: Times Square at midnight from a window above it, foggy Dumbo, ferries and One World Observatory, then Philadelphia and Washington, DC.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · December 2022 – January 2023</p>
  <h1>New Year on the East Coast</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">The ball drop from a window above Times Square, then three rivers, two more cities and a week of short winter days.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>7 days · December 29, 2022 – January 4, 2023</strong><br />
    Fly into Newark → New York → train to Philadelphia → train to Washington, DC → fly home<br />
    one ball drop, seen from above · three ferry rides on New Year's Day · 102 floors up at One World Observatory ·
    one giant clothespin · the Capitol and the Supreme Court after dark
  </div>

  <p>
    Some cities you visit for the sights. New York at New Year's you visit for one sight in particular: a glittering
    ball sliding down a flagpole above Times Square while a million people count backwards from ten. We had no
    intention of standing in the street for twelve hours to see it. Instead, through a stroke of planning that still
    feels like cheating, we watched it from a window high above the square, looking straight down at the confetti.
  </p>
  <p>
    That was the centerpiece of a week on the East Coast in the dead of winter. It was four days in New York, with fog
    in Brooklyn, sun on the harbor and a lot of time on ferries, followed by a night in Philadelphia and a night in
    Washington, with the train in between. As with the other trip posts here, everything is reconstructed from the camera roll, which remembers
    where and when every photo was taken far better than we do. Where the photos are silent, I've kept quiet too.
    Where a place has a story, I've added a little of it. Readers of the <a href="/misc/roadtrip-2024">summer 2024
    road trip</a> will recognize some of the scenery; this is the same coast, eighteen months earlier and about sixty
    degrees colder.
  </p>

  <h2>Arrival: a sea of lights</h2>
  <p>
    We flew in on the evening of December 29th. By seven o'clock the plane was descending over eastern Pennsylvania
    and northern New Jersey, and the window filled with that particular view you only get landing in the Northeast at
    night: no darkness anywhere, just one continuous carpet of orange streetlights, broken by the black ribbons of
    rivers and the brighter threads of highways, all the way to the horizon. The wingtip light flashed against it every
    second, like a metronome counting down the last few minutes of the flight. We landed at Newark and took the airport
    bus across the river into Manhattan.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'plane-approach.webp'} alt="Night view from a plane window: the wingtip light glowing over a vast carpet of city lights." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Descending over New Jersey, December 29th, a little after 7 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    By ten that night we were at the 42nd Street end of <strong>Times Square</strong>, which hits you all at once.
    One minute you are on an ordinary Manhattan street; the next you are standing in a canyon of light so bright it casts shadows at ten at night. Every surface is a screen. A drag queen
    the height of a house advertised a new season on one wall; a cheeseburger the size of a bus glistened on another.
    Above them the numerals of 1540 Broadway marched across the façade in colored lights. Two days before New Year's
    Eve, the square was already being rebuilt for the big night: stages going up on scaffolding, metal barricades
    stacked along the curbs, banners announcing "New Year's Eve Times Square 2023". The crowd was dense and slow and in
    no hurry at all, which is what a crowd becomes when it has nowhere to go and is already where it wanted to be.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ts-billboards.webp'} alt="Times Square at night: giant illuminated billboards on every building, a stage being built, and crowds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Times Square on our first night, two days before the big one.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>December 30: Midtown on foot</h2>
  <p>
    The next afternoon we did what every visitor does in the last week of December: walked Midtown, slowly, in a
    crowd. A city that is famous for walking fast slows to a shuffle around the holidays, because half of the people on
    Fifth Avenue are standing still, looking up.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-1230.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-1230.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Day 2, December 30: Rockefeller Center, St. Patrick's, the Public Library, the Empire State Building, the Farley Building, the USS Intrepid and back to Times Square." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 2: Midtown on foot, out to the Hudson and back.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    We started at <strong>Rockefeller Center</strong> at a quarter to four, with the sun already low. The tree was up
    and lit, a huge Norway spruce strung with tens of thousands of lights and topped with a crystal star, standing
    beneath the Art Deco slab of 30 Rockefeller Plaza. The tradition goes back to 1931, when construction workers
    building the complex pooled their money for a small tree on the muddy site; the official version began two years
    later, and the tree has been the city's Christmas centerpiece ever since. A row of white flags snapped around the
    rink below. Across Fifth Avenue, <strong>St. Patrick's Cathedral</strong> raised its twin spires through the bare
    branches. James Renwick Jr.'s Gothic Revival cathedral opened in 1879, when this stretch of Fifth Avenue was
    still the edge of town, and the spires were added a few years later. Today it is surrounded by glass and
    steel towers, and somehow manages to look like the older, calmer sibling of all of them.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'rock-center-tree.webp'} alt="The Rockefeller Center Christmas tree, lit, in front of the tall stone tower of 30 Rockefeller Plaza under a pale sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The tree at Rockefeller Center.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'st-patricks.webp'} alt="The twin Gothic spires of St. Patrick's Cathedral seen through bare tree branches, with crowds on Fifth Avenue." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>St. Patrick's Cathedral, across Fifth Avenue.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Heading south on Fifth Avenue, the light changed by the minute. In late December the sun sets before five, and for
    the last half hour it skims in low from the west, along the cross streets. It hits only the tops of the towers,
    so that the upper floors burn copper and gold while the street below has already gone blue. A pale half-moon was
    already up between two buildings. At 42nd Street we reached the <strong>New York Public Library</strong>, the
    great Beaux-Arts marble building by Carrère and Hastings that opened in 1911, with its two stone lions, Patience
    and Fortitude, guarding the steps. The steps were crowded with people resting their feet. Behind them, an Art Deco
    tower caught the last of the sun and glowed like it was lit from inside.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fifth-ave-moon.webp'} alt="Looking up between Midtown towers, one lit gold by the setting sun, with a half moon in the blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A half-moon over Midtown at four in the afternoon.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'nypl-golden-hour.webp'} alt="The New York Public Library's marble façade and a stone lion in shadow, with an Art Deco tower glowing orange in the late sun behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Public Library, with a tower behind catching the last light.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    A few blocks further, at 34th Street, the <strong>Empire State Building</strong> appears the way it usually
    does when you're actually next to it: not as the elegant needle of the postcards but as a sheer limestone cliff
    going straight up, too tall to take in at once. It was built in an astonishing thirteen and a half months and opened
    in 1931, at the bottom of the Depression. For forty years it was the tallest building in the world. From the
    sidewalk you mainly notice the setbacks, the stepped "wedding cake" profile that New York's 1916 zoning law
    required so that light could still reach the street.
  </p>
  <p>
    After a quick bite near Herald Square, we walked west in the dark to 8th Avenue and the <strong>James A. Farley
    Building</strong>, the city's main post office since 1914. It is a full two blocks of Corinthian columns lit
    gold, with the old postal motto carved along the frieze: "Neither snow nor rain nor heat nor gloom of night stays
    these couriers from the swift completion of their appointed rounds." It was designed by McKim, Mead &amp; White to
    match their Pennsylvania Station across the street. When that station was demolished in 1963, the loss was so
    widely mourned that it helped start the historic-preservation movement in America. Six decades later, the Farley
    Building was given a second life as Moynihan Train Hall, so a piece of the grand station finally came back.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'empire-state.webp'} alt="The Empire State Building seen from directly below, a pale stone cliff against a blue evening sky, with a red traffic light." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Empire State Building from its own sidewalk.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'farley-building.webp'} alt="The James A. Farley Building's long row of Corinthian columns lit warm gold at night, with towers behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Farley Building on 8th Avenue, now also Moynihan Train Hall.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Then we kept going west, past a red-brick church on 42nd Street wrapped in Christmas lights and the neon Circle Line
    sign at the piers, all the way to the Hudson. The river at night is a different city: wide, black and quiet, with
    the wind coming straight off the water and the lights of New Jersey strung along the far bank. Moored at Pier 86 is
    the <strong>USS <em>Intrepid</em></strong>, an aircraft carrier commissioned in 1943 that fought in the Pacific,
    later picked up Mercury and Gemini astronauts after splashdown, and has been a museum since 1982. At night it is lit
    from below in pale gray and blue, with jets parked on its deck, and the whole enormous hull is mirrored in the water.
    Seen from the railing, it looks less like a ship than a building that has decided to float.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'intrepid.webp'} alt="The aircraft carrier USS Intrepid lit up at night at its Hudson River pier, with aircraft on deck, reflected in the dark water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The USS <em>Intrepid</em> at Pier 86, a little after 7 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    We walked back east along 44th Street into the Theater District, past mounted police officers sitting high above
    the crowd on their horses, and came out at the north end of Times Square at twenty to eight. The crowd had doubled
    since the night before. At the far end of the square, above everything, stood the narrow tower of One Times
    Square, wrapped from top to bottom in screens. That's where the ball would drop in a little over forty-eight hours.
    It has been dropping there since the New Year of 1908, when the New York Times, the building's original owner,
    replaced its fireworks with an illuminated ball of iron and wood. The current one is twelve feet across and covered
    in nearly three thousand Waterford crystal triangles.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ts-ball-tower.webp'} alt="Times Square at night looking toward One Times Square, a narrow tower covered in screens, with a New Year's Eve 2023 banner and crowds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>One Times Square, where the ball waits.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ts-crowds.webp'} alt="Huge glowing billboards over a dense crowd in Times Square, with a stage tower and a clock tower in the background." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>December 30th, 7:40 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2>December 31: fog, then midnight</h2>
  <p>
    New Year's Eve began in a cloud. Rain had come through, and by the afternoon a thick fog had settled over the East
    River. We took the subway to York Street, in Brooklyn, and walked into <strong>Dumbo</strong> a little after four.
    The name stands for "Down Under the Manhattan Bridge Overpass", coined in the 1970s by artists who had moved into
    the empty warehouses here and hoped an unappealing name would keep developers away. It did not work: the warehouses
    are now some of the most expensive lofts in the city.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-1231.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-1231.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Day 3, December 31: Dumbo and Brooklyn Bridge Park, Downtown Brooklyn, Fort Greene, then back to Times Square for midnight." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 3: fog in Dumbo, oysters in Fort Greene, and back across the river for midnight.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The most famous view in Dumbo is from the corner of Washington and Water Streets. Looking down Washington Street,
    the cobbles run between two rows of red-brick warehouses, and the Manhattan Bridge fills the gap at the end. On a
    clear day the Empire State Building is perfectly framed beneath its arch. On this day the fog had taken the Empire
    State Building, the far tower and the top of the near one, and the bridge seemed to rise out of the street and
    simply stop in midair. It was, if anything, better. A crowd stood in the middle of the street, umbrellas up,
    waiting for gaps in the traffic to take the same photo we did. Down by the water, the bridge passed overhead with a
    low rumble of subway trains, its steel deck vanishing into gray in both directions.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dumbo-manhattan-bridge.webp'} alt="The Manhattan Bridge tower framed between red-brick warehouses on Washington Street in Dumbo, its top lost in fog." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Washington Street, the classic view, with the fog taking the top half.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'manhattan-bridge-below.webp'} alt="The underside of the Manhattan Bridge's steel deck and tower in fog, over a park building." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Manhattan Bridge from underneath.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    A few minutes along the waterfront, <strong>Brooklyn Bridge Park</strong> looks out at the
    <strong>Brooklyn Bridge</strong>. In the fog it was a ghost of itself. The near tower stood dark and solid, the
    cables fanned out toward the river, and then the whole thing faded, so that the far tower, on the Manhattan side,
    was only a faint gray outline. Manhattan itself had disappeared entirely. Where the skyline should have been there
    was nothing at all, just white. A work barge with a yellow excavator sat in the water below, and the river lapped
    on the rocks. When the bridge opened in 1883 it was the longest suspension bridge in the world, and its granite
    towers were among the tallest structures in the city. It took fourteen years to build. John Roebling, who designed it,
    died of an injury before construction began; his son Washington took over and was crippled by caisson disease,
    and Washington's wife Emily effectively ran the project for years, relaying his instructions to the engineers.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'brooklyn-bridge-fog.webp'} alt="The Brooklyn Bridge fading into thick fog over the East River, a work barge with an excavator in front." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Brooklyn Bridge, with Manhattan somewhere behind it.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Fog does strange things to sound. The traffic on the bridges becomes a soft, distant hum; the water is louder than
    you'd expect; voices carry along the path. The little pebble beach under the bridges was gray on gray: dark rocks,
    pale stones, flat water and a white sky, with a few people standing at the edge looking out at where the city was
    supposed to be. Back in the streets, the cobblestones shone with rain, the old warehouse windows were starting to
    glow, and at the end of one narrow block a new glass tower stood with its top lost in the cloud.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dumbo-beach-fog.webp'} alt="A pebble beach and rocks on the East River in fog, with the Brooklyn Bridge faint in the mist." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The pebble beach under the bridges.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dumbo-cobbles.webp'} alt="A wet cobblestone street in Dumbo at dusk between tall warehouse buildings, a curved glass tower at the end." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wet cobbles in Dumbo, a little before dark.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    By five the light was gone, and Downtown Brooklyn's new towers rose into the fog with their lit windows smeared
    into halos. We ended up in Fort Greene for an early New Year's Eve dinner, and the evening's indulgence was a plate
    of oysters on ice, which feels like the correct way to end a year, especially a year you have spent mostly not
    eating oysters.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'downtown-brooklyn-fog.webp'} alt="Downtown Brooklyn towers at night, their tops disappearing into fog, behind bare branches." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Downtown Brooklyn, towers into the cloud.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'oysters.webp'} alt="A plate of oysters on crushed ice with lemon and a small bowl of sauce." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The last dinner of 2022.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Midnight, from above</h3>
  <p>
    Then we went back to Manhattan, and up. From our window that evening, Times Square looked like a stage set seen
    from the fly tower. Straight below were the red steps of the TKTS booth in Duffy Square, the pens full of people,
    the broadcast stage glowing purple and the white roofs of the production tents. The screens were at eye level
    instead of overhead, and for the first time you could see them as what they are: a wall of rectangles, each fighting
    to be the brightest. The people in the pens had been there since the afternoon. Once you leave a pen on New Year's Eve there is no getting back in, and there is nowhere to buy food or find a
    bathroom, which is why watching from upstairs felt like cheating.
  </p>
  <p>
    At 11:59 the ball began its descent, sixty seconds down its pole, while the whole square counted down with it.
    At midnight the confetti went up. It is
    said to be about three thousand pounds of it, thrown by hand from the rooftops, and from above it looks like the
    whole square has suddenly been snowed on in color. My one photo of the moment, taken at 12:01 through a window
    wet with rain, is tilted and blurred, and it is the most accurate photo I own of how midnight felt.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'nye-from-above.webp'} alt="Looking straight down on Times Square on New Year's Eve: billboards, the red TKTS steps, a lit stage and packed crowd pens." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>8:42 pm: the crowd in its pens, three hours to go.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'nye-midnight.webp'} alt="A tilted, rain-speckled view straight down onto Times Square at midnight, confetti on the street and stage." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>12:01 am, January 1st, 2023.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2>January 1: two rivers and a tower</h2>
  <p>
    New Year's Day was the opposite of the day before: cold, bright and perfectly clear, the sky a hard, deep blue.
    Times Square at half past ten in the morning was strewn with confetti and quieter than we'd ever seen it, with a
    "Happy New Year 2023" sign on the screens and the cleaning crews already at work. We spent the whole day on the
    water and above it.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0101.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0101.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Day 4, January 1: a ferry down the Hudson from Midtown to Battery Park City, One World Observatory, the Oculus, Brooklyn Heights, a ferry to Pier 11, Wall Street and the Battery, then a ferry up the East River to Roosevelt Island." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 4: by ferry down the Hudson, up the tower, across to Brooklyn, and up the East River at night.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Down the Hudson</h3>
  <p>
    We walked west to the river and caught a ferry from a pier in Midtown, all bright green railings and red-and-white
    boats, with the cliffs of New Jersey across the water. Taking a ferry down the west side of Manhattan is one of the
    best tricks in the city: you get the view the sightseeing boats sell, at commuter speed, sitting among people
    who are mostly not looking out the window.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'newyear-morning.webp'} alt="Times Square on New Year's morning under a blue sky, a 'Happy New Year 2023' sign on One Times Square and confetti in the air." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Times Square, 10:40 am on New Year's Day.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ferry-pier.webp'} alt="A ferry pier on the Hudson with bright green railings and a red-and-white ferry, under a deep blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The ferry pier on the West Side.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    From the water, <strong>Hudson Yards</strong> looks like a cluster of glass crystals that grew out of the rail
    yards, which is close to the truth: the whole district is built on a platform over a working train yard. It was the
    largest private development in American history, and it went up in a decade on a site that had been tracks and
    parking lots. Across the river, the towers of <strong>Jersey City</strong> lined up along the shore, a skyline
    most people only ever see from Manhattan and never think to name. The Hudson was choppy and brown-green, and the
    winter sun came off it so hard it was difficult to look at.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hudson-yards-water.webp'} alt="The glass towers of Hudson Yards seen from a ferry on the Hudson River under a clear blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hudson Yards from the river.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'jersey-city.webp'} alt="The Jersey City skyline across the Hudson River on a bright winter day." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Jersey City, across the water.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The ferry let us off at <strong>Battery Park City</strong>, the neighborhood built on landfill from the
    excavation of the original World Trade Center. Before the 1970s, everything west of West Street here was river.
    Walking inland, we passed the <strong>Irish Hunger Memorial</strong>, a half-acre of Irish hillside, complete with
    a ruined stone cottage brought over from County Mayo, lifted up on a slab of stone and concrete. Behind it rose
    the gently curved glass of 200 West Street, and beyond that <strong>One World Trade Center</strong>, which from
    its base is less a building than a vanishing point. Its height, 1,776 feet to the tip of the spire, is a deliberate
    nod to the year of the Declaration of Independence. The tower's footprint is a square, and its corners are beveled
    as it rises, so its cross-section turns into an octagon halfway up and back into a square, rotated 45 degrees, at
    the top. That is why its edges seem to twist when you look straight up.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'irish-hunger-memorial.webp'} alt="The stone-topped Irish Hunger Memorial in front of a curved glass office tower and the spire of One World Trade Center." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Battery Park City: the Irish Hunger Memorial, 200 West Street and One World Trade.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'one-wtc.webp'} alt="One World Trade Center seen from its base, a tapering glass tower against a deep blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>One World Trade Center, looking straight up.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>One World Observatory</h3>
  <p>
    At half past one we went up. The elevators to <strong>One World Observatory</strong> climb 102 floors in under a
    minute, and while your ears pop, the walls show a time-lapse of the island below being built, from forest to Dutch
    village to the present skyline, five hundred years in sixty seconds. The observatory itself is on floors 100 to 102,
    about 1,250 feet up, and on a day like that the visibility seemed unlimited.
  </p>
  <p>
    To the south was the best view of the trip. The afternoon sun was low over New Jersey and shining straight at us
    off the harbor, so the whole of Upper New York Bay turned to beaten silver, rippled by the wakes of ferries. In
    the middle of it, small and dark and unmistakable, stood the <strong>Statue of Liberty</strong> on her island,
    in silhouette, with the ferries' wakes drawing white lines around her. From this height she
    is tiny, and somehow that makes her more moving, not less.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'obs-liberty.webp'} alt="Upper New York Bay from One World Observatory, the water shining silver in the afternoon sun, the Statue of Liberty in silhouette on her island." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The harbor from One World Observatory, with the Statue of Liberty in silhouette.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    To the north, all of Manhattan lies spread out like a model of itself. The West Side piers reach into the
    Hudson like the teeth of a comb, the street grid runs up the island in parallel lines, and the tower's own
    shadow stretches enormously across Tribeca and SoHo, a long dark finger laid across the rooftops. From up here you
    understand the grid as a single idea: the Commissioners' Plan of 1811 laid out these streets across what was then
    mostly farmland, two centuries before most of the buildings existed.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'obs-west-side.webp'} alt="Aerial view north along Manhattan's West Side, the Hudson River piers on the left and Hudson Yards in the distance." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The West Side piers and Hudson Yards, from 100 floors up.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'obs-shadow.webp'} alt="Aerial view of Lower Manhattan rooftops with the long shadow of One World Trade Center falling across them, Midtown in the distance." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The tower's shadow across Tribeca.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    To the east, the <strong>Brooklyn</strong> and <strong>Manhattan</strong> bridges crossed the river side by side,
    the same two bridges that had been swallowed by fog twenty-four hours earlier, now sharp in every cable. The
    Gothic crown of the Woolworth Building, the tallest in the world when it opened in 1913, stood just below us. And
    looking up the island, Midtown stood clear on the horizon: the Empire State Building in the middle, and around it
    the new "pencil" towers along 57th Street, Billionaires' Row, so thin that from this distance they look like
    toothpicks someone stuck into the skyline.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'obs-bridges.webp'} alt="Aerial view east over Lower Manhattan to the Brooklyn and Manhattan bridges crossing the East River, with Brooklyn beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Brooklyn and Manhattan bridges, fog-free.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'obs-midtown.webp'} alt="The Midtown Manhattan skyline from One World Observatory, the Empire State Building at center and slender supertall towers around it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Midtown on the horizon, Empire State in the middle.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Back on the ground, we cut through the <strong>Oculus</strong>, the transit hub Santiago Calatrava built over
    the World Trade Center PATH station, which opened in 2016. Calatrava said the design was inspired by a bird
    released from a child's hands. Inside, white steel ribs rise on both sides and meet at a long skylight along the
    spine, so the hall feels like the inside of a whale's ribcage, if the whale were very clean and full of shops.
    That week the balconies were hung with holiday garlands.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'oculus.webp'} alt="The white ribbed interior of the Oculus transit hub, with holiday garlands on the balconies and people crossing the marble floor." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Inside the Oculus.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Brooklyn Heights, and the East River at sunset</h3>
  <p>
    Next we crossed to <strong>Brooklyn Heights</strong> for pizza, and then walked out onto the
    <strong>Promenade</strong> just before four. The Promenade is a third of a mile of benches and railings
    cantilevered over the Brooklyn-Queens Expressway. It was a compromise of the 1940s: Robert Moses wanted to run the
    highway straight through the neighborhood, residents fought back, and the result was a stacked deck, with the
    highway below and a public walk on top. From it, Lower Manhattan's skyline stood across the river in full
    golden-hour light, every tower a slightly different shade of amber, with One World Trade rising over the middle.
    The Promenade was crowded with people who had had the same idea. Nobody seemed to mind.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'promenade-skyline.webp'} alt="The Lower Manhattan skyline across the East River in golden late-afternoon light, seen from Brooklyn Heights, piers below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lower Manhattan from the Brooklyn Heights Promenade, just before four.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Then came our second ferry of the day, back across the East River to Pier 11 at Wall Street, and we stepped off at
    exactly the right moment. The sun was setting behind Manhattan, so it lit Brooklyn from the front: the old brick warehouses and
    apartment towers of the Heights glowed a deep orange, as if they were heated from inside, against a sky that was
    going pale blue and lilac. Turning the other way, the Brooklyn Bridge stood dark against a sunset sky, with gulls
    circling overhead. The water was rough and steel-blue, and those ten minutes on
    the pier were the most beautiful of the trip.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'brooklyn-golden.webp'} alt="Brooklyn Heights glowing orange in the setting sun across the East River, a tour boat on the water below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Brooklyn, lit by the sunset behind us.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'brooklyn-bridge-sunset.webp'} alt="The Brooklyn Bridge against a golden sunset sky over choppy water, with gulls flying overhead." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Brooklyn Bridge from the Pier 11 waterfront, 4:26 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Wall Street and the Battery</h3>
  <p>
    The Financial District on the evening of a public holiday is a canyon with no traffic. We walked up from the
    pier into streets so narrow and so tall that the sky was a thin strip far overhead, and on one corner a striped
    orange-and-white stack was venting a column of steam into the cold air. That's Con Edison's steam system, the
    largest in the country, which has been piping steam under Manhattan since 1882 to heat and cool hundreds of
    buildings; the striped stacks lift the vapor from
    the street up above head height. At the end of
    <strong>Wall Street</strong>, exactly where it should be, stood the dark Gothic spire of <strong>Trinity
    Church</strong>. The current church dates from 1846, when its spire was the tallest point in the city;
    Alexander Hamilton is buried in its churchyard.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wall-st-steam.webp'} alt="A Financial District street canyon at dusk with steam pouring from an orange-and-white stack beside an old stone building." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Steam on Water Street.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'trinity-wall-st.webp'} alt="Looking down Wall Street between tall stone buildings toward the dark Gothic spire of Trinity Church at dusk." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Trinity Church at the end of Wall Street.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Outside the <strong>New York Stock Exchange</strong>, with a wreath and an American flag hung on its columned
    façade, a towering tree lit the street in red, green and gold. The exchange has been putting up a tree on
    Broad Street for about a century; a sign beside this one advertised the 99th annual lighting. Crowds wandered
    around it taking photos, which on a normal weekday would be impossible: this is not a street you are usually
    allowed to stand still in.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'nyse-tree.webp'} alt="A tall Christmas tree covered in colored lights on Broad Street beside the New York Stock Exchange, with an American flag and stone towers around." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Stock Exchange tree, its 99th year.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Then we reached the very bottom of the island in time for the end of the sunset. At the edge of the Battery, the
    western sky behind the bare trees went suddenly, absurdly orange and pink, the color of a stove ring, with the
    silhouettes of branches black against it and the old stone office buildings around Peter Minuit Plaza
    catching the glow in their windows. Nearby, the Beaux-Arts Battery Maritime Building, the 1909 terminal for the
    ferries to Brooklyn, was lit up in gold under a gray sky. It lasted perhaps ten minutes, and then the sky went
    dark blue and the streetlights took over.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'battery-sunset.webp'} alt="A fiery orange and pink sunset seen through bare tree branches and an iron fence at the southern tip of Manhattan, with a stone building on the right." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sunset at the Battery, 4:42 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Up the East River to Roosevelt Island</h3>
  <p>
    The day's third ferry took us north up the East River in the dark. Somewhere off Long Island City the famous red
    neon <strong>Pepsi-Cola</strong> sign slid past the window, a looping script and a tilted bottle glowing over the
    water. It has stood on the Queens waterfront since 1940, originally on top of a bottling plant; the plant is long
    gone, replaced by apartment towers, but the sign was declared a city landmark in 2016 and kept where it is.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pepsi-cola.webp'} alt="The red neon Pepsi-Cola sign glowing at night on the Long Island City waterfront, seen from a ferry, its light reflected in the water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Pepsi-Cola sign, Long Island City, through the ferry window.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    We got off at <strong>Roosevelt Island</strong>, the narrow two-mile strip of land in the middle of the East
    River. For most of the nineteenth century it was Blackwell's Island, the place the city sent people it didn't
    want to see: a penitentiary, a smallpox hospital and an asylum. Now it is a quiet residential island with its own
    aerial tram. Right overhead runs the <strong>Queensboro Bridge</strong>, the great steel cantilever bridge of
    1909, famous in song as the 59th Street Bridge. At night it is outlined in lights against the Midtown towers,
    and while we stood underneath it, a tram cabin slid across the river beside it on its cables, high above the water.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'queensboro-tram.webp'} alt="The lit steel trusses of the Queensboro Bridge at night over Roosevelt Island, a tram cabin crossing in the air, Manhattan towers behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Queensboro Bridge, with a tram cabin crossing beside it.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The tram, which opened in 1976, was meant to be temporary until a subway station arrived; the subway took until
    1989, but the tram stayed because people loved it. We walked south past its station and onto the campus of
    <strong>Cornell Tech</strong>, a graduate school built on the site of an old hospital and opened in 2017,
    where the angular glass Tata Innovation Center stood lit from within on an empty plaza. From the lawn at the
    island's edge, Midtown Manhattan spread across the river in a solid wall of lit windows, with the Chrysler
    Building's crown glittering and the Empire State glowing on top. Facing east instead, the towers of Long Island
    City threw long colored reflections across the black water.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'midtown-from-ri.webp'} alt="The Midtown Manhattan skyline at night across the East River from a dark lawn on Roosevelt Island, the Empire State Building lit at the top." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Midtown from Roosevelt Island.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cornell-tech.webp'} alt="The angular glass Tata Innovation Center on the Cornell Tech campus lit from inside at night, an empty plaza in front." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Cornell Tech, after hours.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    It was six o'clock on New Year's Day, the island was nearly silent, and the only sounds were the traffic humming
    on the bridge overhead and the river slapping at the seawall. After two days of crowds, it was a strange and
    lovely place to end up.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lic-reflections.webp'} alt="The lit towers of Long Island City reflected in the East River at night, the Queensboro Bridge on the left, a railing in front." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Long Island City across the water, from the island's east side.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>January 2: Philadelphia</h2>
  <p>
    On the second day of the year we took the train to <strong>Philadelphia</strong>, down the Northeast Corridor,
    the busy rail line that runs from Boston through New York to Washington. The stretch south of New York was
    electrified by the Pennsylvania Railroad in the 1930s, and today Amtrak covers it in well under two hours, through
    the marshes and factory towns of New Jersey. By half past two we were outside 30th Street Station. Philadelphia is only ninety-odd miles from Manhattan, but it feels like a different
    century of a different country: lower, older, red-brick, built on William Penn's orderly grid between two
    rivers, and much less interested in impressing you.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-philly.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-philly.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Day 5, January 2: from 30th Street Station to City Hall and the Clothespin, Chinatown, Center City at night, the JFK Boulevard bridge and the Penn campus." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 5: by train to 30th Street, then City Hall, Chinatown and Penn after dark.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    It is impossible to miss <strong>City Hall</strong>, because it sits in the exact center of Penn's plan, in the
    middle of the intersection of Broad and Market, and every street seems to lead to it. It is a gigantic Second Empire
    pile of granite and marble, completed in 1901 after thirty years of building, with a tower of 548 feet. It was
    the tallest occupied building in the world for a few years, and it is still one of the tallest masonry
    buildings anywhere. On top stands a 37-foot bronze William Penn by Alexander Milne Calder. For most of the
    twentieth century, a gentlemen's agreement kept every new building in the city lower than the brim of Penn's hat.
    It was broken in 1987, and local legend blames the city's sports teams' long championship drought that followed on
    the "Curse of Billy Penn". The drought ended in 2008, a year after a small Penn figurine was placed on top of the city's
    new tallest tower.
  </p>
  <p>
    Across the street stands one of the city's best jokes: the <strong>Clothespin</strong>, a 45-foot steel
    clothespin by Claes Oldenburg, put up in 1976. If the name sounds familiar from the summer 2024 post, it should:
    Oldenburg is the same sculptor who put a giant dustpan and broom outside the Denver Art Museum. Seen from the right
    angle, the Clothespin's two prongs line up beside City Hall's tower and look like they're about to pinch it.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'philly-city-hall.webp'} alt="Philadelphia City Hall, an enormous ornate Second Empire building with a tall clock tower topped by a statue, under a gray sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Philadelphia City Hall under a winter sky.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'clothespin.webp'} alt="Claes Oldenburg's giant black steel Clothespin sculpture standing in front of Philadelphia City Hall." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Oldenburg's <em>Clothespin</em>, sizing up City Hall.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    A few blocks northeast is Chinatown. We stopped there before five for a bowl of
    shaved ice heaped with mango and strawberries, which in January is either a mistake or a statement, and which was
    delicious either way.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'philly-dessert.webp'} alt="A glass bowl of shaved ice topped with chopped mango and strawberries on a wooden table." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A summer dessert in January, Chinatown.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    By six it was full dark and the city had changed character. The air was damp and misty, and every streetlight
    threw long rays of light, so Center City looked like a film set lit for a noir. Above a parking lot on
    Arch Street, City Hall's tower appeared over a row of old buildings, its clock faces glowing yellow and Penn lit
    up on top. Below it was a huge mural of flowers and butterflies. Philadelphia has more public murals than any other
    city in America, thousands of them, most painted through the city's Mural Arts Program, which began in 1984 as an
    anti-graffiti project. On the next corner, a blue signpost pointed toward the Academy of Natural Sciences, the
    Franklin Institute and the Philadelphia Museum of Art, all lined up along the Benjamin Franklin Parkway. Above it the
    towers of Liberty Place rose into the haze, outlined in white lights.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'philly-mural.webp'} alt="City Hall's lit clock tower rising behind old buildings at night, above a large colorful mural of flowers and butterflies." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>City Hall's tower over a Center City mural.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'philly-signpost.webp'} alt="A blue signpost to the Academy of Natural Sciences, Franklin Institute and Philadelphia Museum of Art under lit skyscrapers on a misty night." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Center City at night, signposts to the museums.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    We walked west along John F. Kennedy Boulevard and crossed the Schuylkill River on its bridge. Looking along the river,
    the stone arches of the next bridge were outlined in strings of white lights, doubled into full circles by their
    reflections in the black water, and beyond them 30th Street Station's long colonnaded block glowed gold, with the
    new towers of University City lit up behind. The station opened in 1933, a giant of the railroad age, and it's
    still one of the busiest stations in the country.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'schuylkill-night.webp'} alt="The Schuylkill River at night: bridge arches outlined in lights reflected in the water, 30th Street Station glowing gold, towers behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Schuylkill from the JFK Boulevard bridge.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The evening ended on the <strong>University of Pennsylvania</strong> campus. Locust Walk, the brick path through
    the middle of campus, was wet with rain and almost deserted during the winter break. Strings of lights hung between
    the trees like icicles, and old lampposts made pools of yellow on the wet brick. Near the western end of the walk
    stands <em>Covenant</em>, a big red steel sculpture of tilted cylinders by Alexander Liberman, from 1975.
    Generations of Penn students have given it a nickname that I'll leave to your imagination. In the mist and
    lamplight it looked like a huge red creature stepping over the path.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'penn-covenant.webp'} alt="A large red steel sculpture of tilted cylinders straddling a wet brick path on the University of Pennsylvania campus at night, lampposts glowing in the mist." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Alexander Liberman's <em>Covenant</em> on Locust Walk.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>January 3–4: Washington</h2>
  <p>
    The next day we moved on to <strong>Washington, DC</strong>, by train again, on the last leg of the Corridor
    into Union Station. The camera roll skips the ride entirely and picks up again after dark, at the place every visitor ends up at least once: the <strong>United States Capitol</strong>,
    at eight o'clock at night.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-dc.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-dc.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Days 6 and 7, January 3 and 4: the U.S. Capitol and Supreme Court at night, then Georgetown the next morning." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Days 6–7: Capitol Hill at night, Georgetown in the morning, then the flight home.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    We came at it from the East Front plaza, the side where presidents were inaugurated for a century and a half,
    until Ronald Reagan moved the ceremony to the West Front in 1981. At night the dome is floodlit a pale
    green-white against a black sky. It is not stone but cast iron, nearly nine million pounds of it, painted to look like
    marble. It was built during the Civil War to Thomas U. Walter's design, and Lincoln insisted the work continue
    through the war as a sign that the Union would go on. The Statue of Freedom was lifted onto the top in 1863. Below
    the dome, the portico glowed warm behind its columns, the windows of the wings were lit, and the vast plaza in front
    was almost empty: a few guards, a parked car, the sound of our footsteps.
  </p>
  <p>
    The building was busier than it looked. January 3rd, 2023 was the first day of the new Congress, and that
    afternoon the House had gone through three rounds of voting without electing a Speaker. It would take four days and
    fifteen ballots in the end, the longest such contest since before the Civil War. From the plaza, none of this was
    visible; the Capitol just stood there, lit and calm.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'capitol-night.webp'} alt="The floodlit dome and columned east portico of the United States Capitol at night, an American flag flying." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Capitol's East Front, 8:08 pm, January 3rd.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    From further back the whole building fits in the frame: the dome in the middle, the Senate and House wings stretching
    out left and right, a long line of lit windows, and a compass rose set into the dark pavement in the
    foreground. Directly across First Street stands the <strong>Supreme Court</strong>. Until 1935 the Court met in
    borrowed rooms inside the Capitol itself; then Cass Gilbert gave it this white marble temple of its own, with
    sixteen Corinthian columns and "Equal Justice Under Law" carved above them. At night the portico is lit from within
    and the marble looks almost translucent. On either side of the steps sit James Earle Fraser's two seated figures,
    <em>Contemplation of Justice</em> and <em>Authority of Law</em>, looking out into the dark.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'capitol-plaza.webp'} alt="The full length of the U.S. Capitol at night from the East Front plaza, the dome in the center and a compass rose in the pavement." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The whole Capitol from the East Front plaza.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'supreme-court.webp'} alt="The Supreme Court building at night, its white marble columned portico lit from within above broad steps." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Supreme Court, 8:16 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Georgetown, early</h3>
  <p>
    The last morning of the trip, January 4th, began early in <strong>Georgetown</strong>, with a baguette
    sandwich and a chocolate éclair at eight in the morning. Georgetown is older than Washington itself: it was a
    tobacco port on the Potomac, founded in 1751, four decades before the federal city was laid out next door, and
    its steep streets of brick row houses still feel like a separate town. An hour later we were looking out from a
    rooftop over the neighborhood toward the river, on a gray morning with a band of pale gold along the horizon.
    Below were flat brick roofs and bare trees. Beyond them the Potomac ran wide and still, and on the far bank, in
    Virginia, stood the office towers of Rosslyn, lined up like a second skyline.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'georgetown-breakfast.webp'} alt="A baguette sandwich with mozzarella and greens and a chocolate éclair on paper plates." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Breakfast in Georgetown.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'georgetown-view.webp'} alt="A gray morning view from a rooftop over Georgetown brick roofs and bare trees to the Potomac River and the towers of Rosslyn beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Across the Potomac to Rosslyn, January 4th.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    That rooftop is the last photo. We flew home from DC, but the camera roll doesn't say how the rest of the day
    went, so I'll leave the trip where the photos do: looking across a river on a cold, quiet morning.
  </p>

  <h2>Looking back</h2>
  <p>
    The odd thing about a winter trip is how much of it happens in the dark. The sun set before five every day, and
    more than half of these photos were taken by streetlight, neon or floodlight. Yet it rarely felt gloomy. A city in
    winter turns its lights on early, and the East Coast at the turn of the year was all lights: Christmas trees in
    the plazas, the billboards of Times Square, a neon sign over the river, a lit dome on a hill.
  </p>
  <p>
    Looking back, the week had a shape I didn't notice at the time. It started with the loudest place in America at
    its loudest moment, and it ended on a quiet rooftop in Georgetown. In between were the fog in Dumbo, which hid
    everything, and the clear sky over the harbor the next day, which showed everything. And it had a lot of rivers.
    We spent New Year's Day crossing the Hudson and the East River by ferry, then saw the Schuylkill under lights and
    the Potomac at dawn. All four of these cities grew up on their rivers, and seen from the water, each one finally
    makes sense.
  </p>
  <p>
    As for midnight: we can say we've seen the ball drop, from above, without standing in a pen for twelve hours. From up there,
    the confetti really does look like snow. Once is probably enough. Then again,
    that's what everyone says.
  </p>
